       "rows": [
        [
         "0",
         "Overfitting",
         "A situation in which a machine-learning model fits noise or spurious patterns in the training data, performing well on that data but generalizing poorly to new, unseen examples.",
         "Overfitting is Memorizing the Textbook Instead of Understanding the Concepts.:\nImagine a student who memorizes an entire textbook word-for-word before an exam. During the test, they can perfectly answer questions that come directly from the book, reciting exact passages. However, when faced with questions that ask them to apply concepts in new situations or explain ideas in their own words, they struggle completely.\n\nThe student (the model) has \"memorized\" the textbook (training data) so precisely that they've learned not just the important concepts but also irrelevant details like specific wording and examples (the noise). They've focused so much on remembering every detail that they missed the underlying principles.\n\nWhen test questions match the textbook exactly (like the training data), they excel. But for questions that require understanding and application (like new, unseen examples), they perform poorly because they've memorized rather than understood.",
         "val_videos/val_0.mp4",
         "2",
         "2",
         "2",
         "1",
         "3",
         "1"
        ],
        [
         "1",
         "Transfer learning",
         "A technique where knowledge gained from training on one task or domain is reused to accelerate or improve learning on a different but related task.",
         "Transfer Learning is Piano Practice Progression.:\nImagine you're learning to play the piano. Instead of starting from scratch with a complex classical piece, you first master basic scales and chord progressions.\n\nWhen you later approach that classical piece, you're not starting from zero - you're transferring your knowledge of notes, finger positioning, and rhythm from your earlier practice. The new piece is different, but your foundational skills make learning it much faster and more effective.\n\nIn transfer learning:\n- The basic piano skills represent the knowledge gained from the initial training task\n- The classical piece represents the new, related task\n- Your ability to learn the new piece faster represents the accelerated learning\n- The connections you make between familiar patterns and new ones represent how the model adapts pre-learned features\n- The fact that both tasks involve piano playing represents how the domains must be related for transfer to work effectively\n\nJust as a pianist doesn't need to relearn how to read music for each new piece, a model using transfer learning doesn't need to relearn basic patterns it has already mastered.",
         "val_videos/val_1.mp4",
         "1",
         "2",
         "1",
         "1",
         "1",
         "2"
        ],
        [
         "2",
         "Universal Design for Learning (UDL)",
         "A framework of principles, derived from cognitive science, for designing flexible learning environments that are accessible and effective for all learners. It encompasses both physical spaces—such as wheelchair-accessible desks, assistive technologies, and collaborative areas—and curriculum design that accommodates diverse interests, abilities, and prior knowledge.",
         "UDL is an Inclusive Playground.:\nImagine a playground designed for all children to enjoy. This playground doesn't just have one type of equipment - it has multiple options that serve the same purpose but in different ways:\n\nInstead of just one path to the slide platform, there are stairs, a gentle ramp, and climbing holds.\n\nThe playground offers various seating options - benches at different heights, some with backs, others without.\n\nThere are quiet corners with books for those who need a break from sensory stimulation, alongside active zones with musical elements and spinning equipment.\n\nInstructions are provided through pictures, words, and even tactile symbols.\n\nIn this playground, every child can participate and learn through play, regardless of their abilities or preferences. Some might take the ramp while others climb, some might enjoy the sensory panels while others prefer the swings - but all are engaged in the fundamental activity of play and discovery.\n\nUDL is like this inclusive playground for learning. Instead of forcing all students through the same entrance, using the same equipment, in the same way, it creates multiple pathways to engage with content, express understanding, and become expert learners.",
         "val_videos/val_2.mp4",
         "1",
         "1",
         "2",
         "1",
         "2",
         "1"
        ],
        [
         "3",
         "object-oriented analysis and design (OOAD)",
         "An approach to analyzing and designing software or business systems that applies object-oriented principles and visual modeling throughout the development process to improve stakeholder communication and product quality.",
         "Object-Oriented Analysis and Design is Architectural Blueprinting.:\nImagine you're planning to build a custom house. Object-oriented analysis and design is like the architectural process:\n\nFirst, you walk around the neighborhood (analysis) identifying different types of houses (classes) - colonial, ranch, Victorian. You notice each house type has specific characteristics (attributes) like number of rooms, and capabilities (methods) like heating systems.\n\nThen in the design phase, you create detailed blueprints. You don't design every house from scratch - you reuse standard components (inheritance) like bathroom layouts. You organize your blueprints into sections (encapsulation) - plumbing, electrical, structural - so different contractors only need to see their relevant parts.\n\nYour blueprints show how rooms connect (relationships between objects) and how systems like plumbing interact with multiple rooms (associations). You might create a modular design where the kitchen can be configured in different ways (polymorphism).\n\nBefore building, you review these blueprints with stakeholders - the family, contractors, inspectors - using visual models everyone can understand, ensuring the final house will meet everyone's needs and building codes.",
         "val_videos/val_3.mp4",
         "2",
         "2",
         "2",
         "0",
         "3",
         "1"
        ],
        [
         "4",
         "symbolic computation",
         "Also called computer algebra, the field that develops algorithms and software for manipulating mathematical expressions and objects in exact symbolic form rather than numerical approximation.",
         "Symbolic Computation is a Precision Chef.:\nImagine a master chef who works with precise recipes instead of approximations. While most cooks might measure \"about a cup\" of flour (numerical approximation), this chef uses exact measurements like \"1 cup plus 2 tablespoons\" (symbolic form).\n\nIn this kitchen:\n- The chef (symbolic computation system) manipulates exact recipe ingredients (mathematical expressions)\n- Instead of saying \"approximately 3.14\" for pi, the chef keeps it as the symbol \"π\" in the recipe\n- When combining ingredients (performing operations), the chef maintains the precise recipe language rather than rounding or estimating\n- If a recipe calls for \"√2 cups of milk,\" the chef doesn't convert it to 1.414 cups but keeps it in its pure symbolic form\n- The chef can simplify complex recipes (like reducing \"x² + 2x + 1\" to \"(x+1)²\") without losing any precision\n\nJust as this chef preserves the exact relationships between ingredients throughout the cooking process, symbolic computation maintains mathematical expressions in their exact form, allowing for perfect precision in mathematical \"recipes.\"",
         "val_videos/val_4.mp4",
         "1",
         "0",
         "2",
         "2",
         "3",
         "2"
        ],
        [
         "5",
         "firewall",
         "A network security system with rules to control incoming and outgoing traffic.",
         "Firewall is a Strict Doorman.:\nImagine you run a fancy restaurant with a strict doorman at the entrance. Anyone wanting to come in or go out must pass through this doorman. He checks a list of rules: who’s on the VIP list, who looks suspicious, and what items are allowed in or out. If someone or something doesn’t fit the rules, the doorman immediately stops them at the door. This way, only approved guests can enter, and only allowed items can leave.",
         "val_videos/val_5.mp4",
