         "3",
         "1"
        ],
        [
         "10",
         "Cloud computing",
         "On-demand delivery of computing resources (compute, storage, networking, software) over the internet with elastic scaling.",
         "Think of your local command line as a remote control for a powerful, shared build server. When you need to compile a large, resource-intensive program, you don't run it on your laptop. Instead, you execute a remote command that sends a request to the build server (the cloud provider).\n\nThe server instantly finds available processing power (compute) and allocates temporary disk space (storage) for your job. If your build needs to fetch libraries, it uses the server's high-speed connection (networking). You get all of this power the moment you run the command (on-demand delivery over the internet).\n\nIf your team suddenly needs to run ten builds at once, the server automatically provisions ten isolated build environments to handle the load, and then tears them down as each build finishes (elastic scaling), ensuring resources are always available but never wasted.",
         "val_videos/val_10.mp4",
         "2",
         "2",
         "0",
         "1",
         "1",
         "0"
        ],
        [
         "11",
         "Internet Protocol (IP) address",
         "A numeric label (IPv4 or IPv6) used to route packets to and from networked devices.",
         "Think of a CPU needing to access a specific byte of data in RAM. The entire RAM is a vast space, but each byte has a unique, numeric memory address (the IP address). When the CPU wants to read or write data, it doesn't scan all of RAM; it sends a request containing that exact memory address. The memory controller then uses this address to go directly to that single location (the networked device) to fetch or place the data (the packet). Without this unique numeric address, the CPU would have no way to specify which byte it wants to communicate with.",
         "val_videos/val_11.mp4",
         "1",
